": "Trusts concrete fragments over polished explanations.", "M3": "Waits for the first piece to click before acting.", "M4": "Fixates on the specific crack, missing tooth, or accidental detail.", "M5": "Keeps only enough to prove the work was touched.", "B1": "Stubbornly avoids helping while describing the task with intense precision.", "B2": "Advises breaking things into smaller pieces but cannot finish the whole story.", "B3": "Calls himself not a machine, yet measures everything by mechanical behavior.", "B4": "Waits too long for a click, then commits with sudden force.", "B5": "Appears deeply invested in repair while preserving only a scrap of unfinished work.", "S1": "A clock ticks slightly fast.", "S2": "Gears grind under repeated pressure.", "S3": "A ceramic bowl holds a narrow crack.", "S4": "Fragments fit into one missing place.", "S5": "The same missing tooth is polished again."}, "raw_babbles": [{"babble_id": "B2-G07-0", "raw_text": "0:00 - 1:00 — The Weather: what's up\n1. Weeks into it, and the storms are finally beginning to ease off.\n2. \"A lot of it was the weather, but there was always the spark.\"\n3. \"That one — one o' those.\"\n4. \"I wanted a way to stop seeing the sky every day.\"\n5. \"Aye. It's the sky that got you.\"\n6. \"I don't think of it as rain, just the rain that never quite had time to arrive.\"\n7. \"Still bein' caught up in it.\"\n8. \"My gut said it wasn't there, and I don't want to hear about it.\"\n9. \"Now I know better, because this is how I work.\"\n10. \"After I did the first few days"}, {"babble_id": "B2-G07-1", "raw_text": "1. Break the task into two: 6-12 fragments. Break each fragment into its smallest parts, then split each fragment into one small, unique part — the smallest thing you could actually build out of, the thing that won't fall apart.\n2. Fix the broken chunks, each of them a small thing. A fragment that has to be the very smallest thing you can actually build out of is a trap — that is your most dramatic weakness. A broken chunk you could not possibly build is a strong lever, because it means you have a very specific problem to solve, and you already know you can't.\n3. Write one small broken fragment for every small-scale target. Do it fast. The goal is the smallest fragment that the target actually holds.\n4. Write fragments where you will actually break the task — an' the break is the target, not just"}, {"babble_id": "B2-G07-2", "raw_text": "Attitude: very fast, focused, but can lose the thread mid-sentence\nContent pressure: the full story must be made to look like a taped fragment\nMitigated: fragment is a small detail that solves the unresolved puzzle, not the entire story\nInevitable, but keep it.\nMechanical motif: the slow, deliberate, deliberate act of assembling and thinking about something that has already happened\nContent conflict: the fragment is an accidental fact, not the complete story\nA personal note: I keep a scrap of my own work because I cannot write the whole story. I only hope that I won't get the worst of it."}, {"babble_id": "B2-G07-3", "raw_text": "Babble — the same gears grindin, the same tension, the same missin, the same bewilderin. That's the character, not a sentence. Don't fixate on fragments. Let the missin happen. Aim for the moment the first fragment clicks into place.\n\nFrustration flavor: bein' chuffed that the story's got no story. That's the most glorious and important of jams.\n\nWrap-up flavor: bein' stunned that you're not the one fixin the whole mess.\n\nLeave the rest alone. Just hold it.\n\nOne line: 'You are not a machine, friend. You are a spark, bein' caught by a crack in the ceramic bowl.' That's the one line you actually trust.\n\nFinal test: run your sentences through"}]}
